            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Latin for thin, then the Latin for leanness."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: rōbustus, rōbusta, rōbustum",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "rōbustus, rōbusta, rōbustum — \"sturdy, strong\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Agricola rōbustus saccum gravem portat. The sturdy farmer carries a heavy sack."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "From rōbur — oak, hardwood: as strong as oak. English robust."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "rōbustus is built on rōbur \"oak, hard wood, strength\" (Lewis & Short; EtymDB): oaken, then sturdy. English robust comes from it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
